  "title": "Telling and Remembering: Complexities in Long‐term Effects of Listeners on Autobiographical Memory",
  "abstract": "Summary The fact that people talk about most of their emotional experiences with others is well established, as are the influences of listeners on the nature of such talk. Established effects of variability in that talk—such as those due to the listener's attentiveness—could also influence later memory, but the evidence directly testing such influences is relatively sparse. We report the results of a study in which participants (n = 64, 30 men) initially narrated a positive or negative experience to a same‐sex friend. The friend was randomly assigned to be either a responsive or a distracted listener, which resulted in changes to the conversational narration that are reported elsewhere. In a second session, approximately 1 month later, participants were asked to recall the same experience. In this study, we examined whether event valence and listener condition influenced the retention of information from the session 1 narrative at the follow‐up session, as well as the consistency of the session 2 narratives with the session 1 narrative. Prior findings linking more attentive listening to higher retention and consistency were partially replicated in the present study but were moderated by gender (retention) and event valence (consistency). Broader implications of these findings are discussed.",
  "year": 2015,
  "venue": "Applied Cognitive Psychology",
